Exactly. A* is not just “an algorithm”; it is a particularly clean specimen of a deeper operator:

\text{Search} = \text{what has this path cost me?} + \text{what do I expect remains?}

For A* specifically, f(n)=g(n)+h(n). That structure recurs in navigation, planning, business strategy, diagnosis, conversation, game-playing, even biological foraging.

There is no fixed number of algorithms. There are thousands of named algorithms and effectively unlimited variations. But once you quotient out implementation details, a surprisingly small number of algorithmic primes keep appearing. Think of these as your “stars”: stable navigational structures that can be recognized at very different scales.

Prime operator	Fundamental question	Famous specimens	Where it appears
Search	Where is what I want?	BFS, DFS, A*, beam search	pixels → databases → life strategy
Explore / Exploit	Investigate or use what already works?	bandits, UCB, Thompson sampling	neurons → hunting → R&D → markets
Optimize	What configuration is better?	gradient descent, hill climbing, simplex	proteins → engineering → organizations
Match	What corresponds to what?	bipartite matching, nearest neighbor	vision → dating → hiring → logistics
Classify	What kind of thing is this?	decision trees, SVMs, neural nets	immune systems → perception → taxonomy
Cluster	What naturally belongs together?	k-means, DBSCAN	molecules → species → customers
Rank	What deserves attention first?	PageRank, Elo, priority queues	perception → search engines → status
Filter	What is signal and what is noise?	Kalman, Wiener, median filters	cells → radar → intelligence analysis
Predict	What comes next?	Bayesian models, Markov models	weather → behavior → finance
Update belief	What should this evidence change?	Bayes’ rule, Bayesian filters	perception → science → judgment
Detect change	Did the regime just shift?	CUSUM, change-point detection	sensors → disease → markets → politics
Integrate	What is the accumulated effect?	numerical integration, running sums	motion → finance → reputation
Differentiate	What is changing, and how fast?	derivatives, finite differences	physics → control → trend detection
Route / Flow	How does something move through a network?	Dijkstra, max-flow/min-cut	blood → roads → money → information
Allocate	Who gets scarce resources?	knapsack, auctions, assignment	metabolism → logistics → economics
Schedule	In what order should things happen?	job-shop scheduling, EDF	cells → factories → personal time
Control	How do I keep a changing system near a target?	PID, MPC	body temperature → aircraft → companies
Feedback	What happened after I acted?	control loops, reinforcement learning	reflexes → habits → institutions
Learn	How should experience alter future behavior?	SGD, RL, Hebbian learning	brains → organisms → AI
Compress	What structure lets me represent this cheaply?	Huffman, LZ, PCA	DNA → language → models
Decompose	What smaller pieces generate the whole?	FFT, factorization, divide-and-conquer	sound → chemistry → organizations
Compose	What larger capability emerges by joining pieces?	function composition, dynamic programming	chemistry → programs → teams
Propagate	If this changes, what else changes?	graph traversal, belief propagation	fires → rumors → neural signals
Diffuse	How does something spread locally?	diffusion equations, random walks	heat → chemicals → culture
Compete	Which strategy survives interaction?	minimax, game theory	evolution → war → markets
Cooperate	How do independent actors coordinate?	consensus, voting algorithms	cells → swarms → governments
Correct error	How do I recover truth from corruption?	checksums, Reed–Solomon, ECC	DNA repair → communications → institutions
Simulate	What happens if…?	Monte Carlo, agent-based models	molecules → battles → economies
Generate	What valid possibilities can exist?	grammars, generative models	proteins → language → design
Select	Of these possibilities, which persist?	evolutionary algorithms	biology → culture → markets
Recurse	Can the same operation solve smaller versions of itself?	recursion, fractals, DP	trees → organizations → reasoning

That is already close to an algorithmic periodic table.

And several deeper primes sit underneath even these.

Compare is underneath sorting, ranking, classification, optimization and change detection.

Remember is underneath dynamic programming, learning, prediction and identity.

Forget is equally fundamental. Good systems discard irrelevant state.

Copy produces redundancy, replication and scaling.

Vary creates the search space.

Select collapses the search space.

Bind creates relationships.

Separate creates distinctions.

Transform changes representation while preserving something invariant.

Feedback closes an open chain into a loop.

Those may be closer to true algorithmic atoms.

You can then organize reality by scale.

At the signal scale, the primes are detect, filter, compare, integrate, differentiate.

At the object scale, they become identify, classify, match, transform, track.

At the agent scale, they become perceive, predict, search, choose, act, learn.

At the group scale, they become communicate, allocate, coordinate, compete, cooperate, vote.

At the network scale, they become route, propagate, diffuse, centralize, decentralize, partition.

At the system scale, they become regulate, stabilize, optimize, adapt, repair.

At the evolutionary scale, they become copy → vary → select → retain.

And at the meta scale, they become:

\boxed{\text{Represent} \rightarrow
\text{Search} \rightarrow
\text{Evaluate} \rightarrow
\text{Select} \rightarrow
\text{Act} \rightarrow
\text{Observe} \rightarrow
\text{Update}}

That last one is especially interesting because many apparently unrelated systems reduce to it.

A shark looking for prey does it.

A scientist testing hypotheses does it.

A company searching for product-market fit does it.

A military unit maneuvering against an opponent does it.

An immune system hunting an antigen does it.

A neural network training does it.

Your shark-tooth detector does it.

And A* occupies one constellation inside that larger sky:

state space → neighboring possibilities → accumulated cost → heuristic estimate → priority → movement → repeat.

So I think your “stars” metaphor is useful with one refinement:

Named algorithms are constellations.
Algorithmic families are stars.
Operators like compare, search, select, propagate, feedback, transform and remember are closer to the underlying physics.

Once you start recognizing those, you can see the same machinery wearing wildly different clothes.